1
00:00:18,792 --> 00:00:24,379
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I was wondering if you could explain, anatta, what is that?

2
00:00:24,859 --> 00:00:26,401
[SPEAKER_00]: Non-self, not-self.

3
00:00:27,783 --> 00:00:29,405
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no self.

4
00:00:29,825 --> 00:00:32,588
[SPEAKER_00]: And what's the point of it?

5
00:00:32,628 --> 00:00:35,372
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do we need to know that, to experience that?

6
00:00:44,061 --> 00:00:46,182
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I think you can start by explaining what it isn't.

7
00:00:47,322 --> 00:00:47,762
[SPEAKER_01]: And, eh...

8
00:00:48,803 --> 00:00:49,903
[SPEAKER_01]: It isn't, eh...

9
00:00:51,464 --> 00:00:54,605
[SPEAKER_01]: It is not a denial of self, as in there is no self.

10
00:00:56,326 --> 00:01:11,191
[SPEAKER_01]: Eh... Because usually people would basically hear that, hear the teaching, anatta, and then, eh... end up kind of naturally jumping into an assumption of, ah, so there is no real self.

11
00:01:11,231 --> 00:01:12,552
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, none of this is real.

12
00:01:14,157 --> 00:01:17,498
[SPEAKER_01]: But denying the problem doesn't actually solve it.

13
00:01:17,538 --> 00:01:23,800
[SPEAKER_01]: And especially doesn't work in this case, because the sense of self, it's not about some metaphysical mistake.

14
00:01:24,060 --> 00:01:30,983
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about a gratuitous sense of ownership of your experience, of your senses.

15
00:01:31,203 --> 00:01:34,524
[SPEAKER_01]: And because of that ownership, the sense of self is generated.

16
00:01:35,824 --> 00:01:38,305
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the sense of self in people's lives, it's not a problem.

17
00:01:38,325 --> 00:01:40,966
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a symptom of an underlying problem of ownership.

18
00:01:43,167 --> 00:01:54,357
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you, if you discern that right order and stop regarding things as mine, and by that I mean being emotionally connected to them, because that's the ownership.

19
00:01:54,417 --> 00:01:58,501
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like you don't care about things that, well, that you don't care.

20
00:01:59,502 --> 00:02:01,724
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if something happens to those things, you won't be affected.

21
00:02:02,265 --> 00:02:04,306
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you saying, oh, that's not mine, makes no difference.

22
00:02:04,327 --> 00:02:06,388
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to apply the attitude of not mine.

23
00:02:07,189 --> 00:02:11,012
[SPEAKER_01]: So, anatta is the most fundamental principle of Buddhist teaching.

24
00:02:11,032 --> 00:02:15,715
[SPEAKER_01]: It refers exactly to that, because there's nothing that you can actually own.

25
00:02:30,315 --> 00:02:35,017
[SPEAKER_01]: In a sense of, eh... that you would be its master, controller, in charge of.

26
00:02:35,637 --> 00:02:39,158
[SPEAKER_01]: And I'm talking about your life, the most fundamental aspects of your experience.

27
00:02:39,778 --> 00:02:45,720
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your feelings, your perceptions, your intentions, are fundamentally given to you, basically.

28
00:02:45,840 --> 00:02:55,163
[SPEAKER_01]: They arise, and because you... you're not... you're not... you don't know any better, you just, eh... assume that they're mine, because they're here, for me.

29
00:02:55,283 --> 00:02:56,124
[SPEAKER_01]: Who else is there?

30
00:02:56,144 --> 00:02:56,784
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just me.

31
00:02:57,564 --> 00:03:02,686
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's the problem with ignorance, because there is no first origin of that mistake.

32
00:03:03,246 --> 00:03:08,428
[SPEAKER_01]: It is a mistake, but there is no first point where that mistake became, before which everything was nice and pure.

33
00:03:09,429 --> 00:03:14,551
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that mistake is a starting point, which means you have to accept that there is a mistake.

34
00:03:14,651 --> 00:03:19,933
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to accept that there is a sense of self, that thinks it owns things.

35
00:03:21,173 --> 00:03:25,955
[SPEAKER_01]: But then you listen to the Buddha's teaching, you read the suttas, and you actually get the sense that it's the other way around.

36
00:03:26,928 --> 00:03:34,338
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because you own things, you keep redetermining that sense of self, as that independent owner of these things.

37
00:03:35,980 --> 00:03:37,742
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's, as I said, it's other way around.

38
00:03:39,164 --> 00:03:41,547
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because you keep engaging in the acts of ownership,

39
00:03:42,922 --> 00:03:44,623
[SPEAKER_01]: That sense of self is being maintained.

40
00:03:45,524 --> 00:03:49,326
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you stop doing that and then you... then the anatta teaching becomes apparent.

41
00:03:49,366 --> 00:03:58,991
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like there is no self, there is no mistake, but you undo the mistake of ownership, so then you can truly say there is not self, as in not my own self.

42
00:03:59,952 --> 00:04:02,734
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that phenomenon of self still remains.

43
00:04:03,514 --> 00:04:04,675
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, it's not mine.

44
00:04:07,810 --> 00:04:10,732
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no, there is no involvement of denial of the sense of self.

45
00:04:11,493 --> 00:04:18,479
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically, eh... accepting it as an arisen phenomena and then undoing the ownership of it.

46
00:04:19,560 --> 00:04:24,363
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can truly say anatta means that that which is taken to be myself, it's not mine.

47
00:04:25,404 --> 00:04:28,006
[SPEAKER_01]: And that experience is the experience of anatta.

48
00:04:29,588 --> 00:04:31,389
[SPEAKER_01]: And another thing is basically, eh...

49
00:04:31,429 --> 00:04:33,331
[SPEAKER_01]: I think we spoke about it before, actually, eh...

50
00:04:33,931 --> 00:04:35,893
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know whether it was recorded or not, but eh...

51
00:04:37,097 --> 00:04:47,023
[SPEAKER_01]: Often people would just quote random science or things like that, just to prove that everything is constantly changing, thus nothing is mine.

52
00:04:47,603 --> 00:04:49,604
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not an answer that the Buddha talks about.

53
00:04:50,165 --> 00:04:53,787
[SPEAKER_01]: It's that personal side of ownership, that emotional connection to it.

54
00:04:54,715 --> 00:05:05,558
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're not proving any anatta if you say, look, this stone over there is comprised of such and such minerals and elements and atoms and neutrons and whatever else.

55
00:05:06,318 --> 00:05:10,239
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, it's not really... it's not really real, it's not really mine.

56
00:05:10,820 --> 00:05:12,960
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't care about it to begin with.

57
00:05:13,020 --> 00:05:14,161
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're not proving anything.

58
00:05:15,601 --> 00:05:20,802
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that sense of ownership, it's not an ideology or a kind of an abstract idea.

59
00:05:20,943 --> 00:05:22,163
[SPEAKER_01]: It's emotional aptitude.

60
00:05:23,183 --> 00:05:32,873
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, try and explain in the same scientific way to your own daughter, or your son, or your child, your baby, or something you're inherently bound with emotionally.

61
00:05:34,182 --> 00:05:41,148
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, see how indifferent you can remain, in fact, of your explanation that it's nothing, it's just a clump of elements and cells.

62
00:05:41,808 --> 00:05:42,309
[SPEAKER_01]: It's pointless.

63
00:05:42,849 --> 00:05:44,530
[SPEAKER_01]: It's completely...

64
00:05:44,751 --> 00:05:47,913
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't even apply on the same domain, such explanation.

65
00:05:48,333 --> 00:05:52,337
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means people use it only for this ideological purpose, to justify their views.

66
00:05:52,957 --> 00:05:58,141
[SPEAKER_01]: But practically, basically, the Buddha's teaching frees you from that emotional dependence.

67
00:05:58,582 --> 00:06:00,063
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't explain things to you.

68
00:06:01,452 --> 00:06:10,058
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you think that anatta is this random, everything is constantly changing, and nothing is mine, well, it isn't, because that doesn't free you from that emotional dependence upon those things.

69
00:06:10,838 --> 00:06:22,726
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to basically find things that you are emotionally dependent upon, and then start undoing the ownership of them, because you see yourself liable to suffering on account of losing them, even if you don't necessarily lose them.

70
00:06:23,266 --> 00:06:28,770
[SPEAKER_01]: And actually, you don't want to wait for them to be gone, to see that you're liable to suffering of them being gone.

71
00:06:28,970 --> 00:06:29,891
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to do it before.

72
00:06:30,781 --> 00:06:31,281
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

73
00:06:31,301 --> 00:06:39,785
[SPEAKER_01]: Anatta refers to that personal attitude of something that's dear to you, something that you are connected with.

74
00:06:40,206 --> 00:06:41,726
[SPEAKER_01]: And I think...

75
00:06:44,968 --> 00:06:50,510
[SPEAKER_01]: I think the etymology of that word, of the Sanskrit nitya, as in nicca...

76
00:06:51,291 --> 00:06:51,551
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry?

77
00:06:51,831 --> 00:06:52,251
[SPEAKER_00]: Anicca.

78
00:06:52,772 --> 00:06:56,894
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, anicca and nicca, as in anicca and nitya in Sanskrit.

79
00:06:56,914 --> 00:06:59,395
[SPEAKER_01]: By the way, it's been a long time ago since I was looking for that, but...

80
00:07:00,647 --> 00:07:07,849
[SPEAKER_01]: There are lots of synonyms with that word, and it can be used basically for a personal, internal dear to me, nitya.

81
00:07:08,470 --> 00:07:10,971
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, basically, anitya would be the opposite of that.

82
00:07:11,811 --> 00:07:17,513
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, you can say it's impermanent, but actually, it probably doesn't do it justice because of

83
00:07:18,273 --> 00:07:29,322
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, today's modern contemporary view is what people think permanence, impermanence, is this kind of almost impersonal debate of physics, of how everything is impermanent, everything is constantly changing.

84
00:07:29,682 --> 00:07:34,145
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's just denial of the existence of the things that you're emotionally bound with.

85
00:07:35,827 --> 00:07:42,652
[SPEAKER_01]: And that denial can provide people with a relief, with a degree of relief, which is why they mistake it for some sort of truth.

86
00:07:43,453 --> 00:07:45,975
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you denying the problem, it covers the problem up.

87
00:07:46,932 --> 00:07:47,933
[SPEAKER_01]: But it doesn't solve it.

88
00:07:48,134 --> 00:07:49,035
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't uproot it.

89
00:07:49,475 --> 00:07:56,865
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, sooner or later, some... well, you'll forget about denying it, or something else will point at it, remind you of it, and you're back to square one.

90
00:07:56,905 --> 00:07:58,548
[SPEAKER_01]: You haven't made... you made no difference.

91
00:08:00,202 --> 00:08:06,685
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why you really want to first accept the problem and stop denying it and stop finding justification for it.

92
00:08:07,705 --> 00:08:19,870
[SPEAKER_01]: And that can be helped if you think that nicca and anicca actually refers to personal and dear and then not taken personally and not dear to me, as in you're indifferent.

93
00:08:20,730 --> 00:08:22,031
[SPEAKER_01]: Emotional equilibrium.

94
00:08:22,611 --> 00:08:25,172
[SPEAKER_01]: And there's that simile that the Buddha gives to the monks.

95
00:08:25,192 --> 00:08:25,492
[SPEAKER_01]: He said,

96
00:08:26,732 --> 00:08:36,357
[SPEAKER_01]: Monks, people, get basically upset, affected by suffering, when things that they are attached to, are gone, are affected.

97
00:08:37,278 --> 00:08:39,339
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they should basically abandon the attachment.

98
00:08:39,659 --> 00:08:44,482
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't say they should basically find the justification for the denial of the existence of those things.

99
00:08:46,611 --> 00:08:49,733
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the simile that he gives is basically when he says...

100
00:08:49,913 --> 00:08:53,315
[SPEAKER_01]: They were sitting in the grove, in the forest, and he said...

101
00:08:53,335 --> 00:08:58,558
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for example, see all these sticks and branches just lying around here, that you're completely indifferent to.

102
00:08:59,579 --> 00:09:04,982
[SPEAKER_01]: Would you be upset if some people come here and start breaking these branches and taking them and burning them?

103
00:09:05,002 --> 00:09:05,763
[SPEAKER_01]: They say, we wouldn't.

104
00:09:06,363 --> 00:09:07,104
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, we wouldn't.

105
00:09:07,144 --> 00:09:07,344
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

106
00:09:07,404 --> 00:09:10,186
[SPEAKER_01]: Because we're completely emotionally indifferent.

107
00:09:10,426 --> 00:09:11,887
[SPEAKER_01]: These branches are nothing to me.

108
00:09:12,707 --> 00:09:18,850
[SPEAKER_01]: He says, well, that's how you should then cultivate the same attitude towards your own body, towards your own senses, towards your own life.

109
00:09:19,931 --> 00:09:25,134
[SPEAKER_01]: But he didn't say that's how you should start dismissing your own body and saying, well, it's not really real and whatever else.

110
00:09:25,995 --> 00:09:28,016
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that would just be obscuring the problem.

111
00:09:29,537 --> 00:09:35,480
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, you want the attitude of completely indifferent.

112
00:09:36,088 --> 00:09:42,153
[SPEAKER_01]: If the modern science were proving that the Buddha was right, it means that every modern scientist who partakes in that view would be at least a Sotāpanna.

113
00:10:06,308 --> 00:10:06,909
[SPEAKER_01]: Or even more.

114
00:10:07,389 --> 00:10:12,313
[SPEAKER_01]: But the fact that they're not, means they don't know what the dhamma is, so they cannot even accidentally prove it.

115
00:10:13,374 --> 00:10:26,885
[SPEAKER_01]: And furthermore, science is directly divorced from any phenomenological proof of the Buddha's teaching, because science inherently negates the existence of observer, of the subject, of the personal point of view.

116
00:10:27,606 --> 00:10:34,732
[SPEAKER_01]: Science inherently operates under the assumption of a public world, independent of all of us, failing to see that that's a point of view.

117
00:10:35,648 --> 00:10:38,789
[SPEAKER_01]: That you are completely bound up with, and you cannot step outside of it.

118
00:10:39,530 --> 00:10:56,537
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, if you engage in science, on account of trying to understand the Buddha's teaching, or if you engage in a scientific view, basically, you're engaging in the view of the denial of the problem of the personality, personal attachment, and craving in regard to your feelings, and all of that.

119
00:10:56,557 --> 00:10:58,818
[SPEAKER_01]: Because then you're just explaining what's happening.

120
00:10:59,158 --> 00:11:01,939
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not really uprooting it through understanding.

121
00:11:01,959 --> 00:11:03,920
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is already what Sotāpanna begins to do.

122
00:11:12,728 --> 00:11:15,490
[SPEAKER_01]: So, similar, actually, to that.

123
00:11:15,570 --> 00:11:16,070
[SPEAKER_01]: I got a book.

124
00:11:16,110 --> 00:11:17,091
[SPEAKER_01]: I wanted to read that bit.

125
00:11:19,012 --> 00:11:20,753
[SPEAKER_01]: It's quite connected to that whole denial.

126
00:11:21,614 --> 00:11:36,543
[SPEAKER_01]: It's when people... Like, there are those similes in the suttas where people would say... Yaran said, well, this body is made out of various organs and parts that, you know, when they break down, there won't be any life left.

127
00:11:36,624 --> 00:11:38,725
[SPEAKER_01]: There won't be any body remaining.

128
00:11:38,785 --> 00:11:41,567
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, there is nothing for me to take there to be mine.

129
00:11:42,375 --> 00:11:46,117
[SPEAKER_01]: But people, again, it's easy to misread that, because the point is subtle.

130
00:11:47,398 --> 00:11:51,400
[SPEAKER_01]: So, people would then think, ah, things that are made out of parts are not real.

131
00:11:52,500 --> 00:12:00,744
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's the famous, the questions of the King Melinda, when he says, well, it's like having a chariot that is made out of parts.

132
00:12:01,725 --> 00:12:04,506
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you take the chariot apart, you have all these random parts.

133
00:12:05,147 --> 00:12:07,508
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, chariot doesn't really exist.

134
00:12:08,612 --> 00:12:17,235
[SPEAKER_01]: But that argument fails to see that a chariot is already a particular functional assembly of parts.

135
00:12:18,255 --> 00:12:21,136
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you don't call it chariot, you can call it an assembly of parts.

136
00:12:21,676 --> 00:12:24,037
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's a completely valid reality and valid truth.

137
00:12:24,137 --> 00:12:25,237
[SPEAKER_01]: And it was never the problem.

138
00:12:26,317 --> 00:12:29,618
[SPEAKER_01]: But because the problem is subtle, the problem is not in the things that you own.

139
00:12:29,858 --> 00:12:32,479
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not in the conventions or your designations.

140
00:12:32,519 --> 00:12:34,660
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem is in your attitude towards it.

141
00:12:35,577 --> 00:12:37,960
[SPEAKER_01]: As in attitude of appropriation and assumption.

142
00:12:38,000 --> 00:12:38,701
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what Buddha said.

143
00:12:38,721 --> 00:12:40,723
[SPEAKER_01]: The five aggregates are not upādāna.

144
00:12:41,304 --> 00:12:45,930
[SPEAKER_01]: Five aggregates are not assumption and attachment, but there is no attachment without the five aggregates.

145
00:12:47,222 --> 00:12:53,943
[SPEAKER_01]: But these questions of King Melinda, he actually goes in explaining that, see, five aggregates can actually be broken into parts.

146
00:12:54,003 --> 00:12:55,904
[SPEAKER_01]: That's... there is no real person there.

147
00:12:56,744 --> 00:13:03,325
[SPEAKER_01]: But that is putting the problem of ownership, putting the problem of attachment into the five aggregates, and then destroying the five aggregates, you destroy the problem.

148
00:13:03,985 --> 00:13:07,566
[SPEAKER_01]: But the Buddha himself said the problem is not in it, it's in regard to it.

149
00:13:08,586 --> 00:13:13,267
[SPEAKER_01]: Desire and lust in regard to the five aggregates, the attachment to your experience is there.

150
00:13:15,471 --> 00:13:16,072
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

151
00:13:16,092 --> 00:13:19,795
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, using that kind of argument just obscures the problem, doesn't solve it.

152
00:13:19,995 --> 00:13:23,439
[SPEAKER_01]: Denial of the reality doesn't solve your attachment to it.

153
00:13:24,439 --> 00:13:27,002
[SPEAKER_01]: Because then, you become attached to the reality of that denial.

154
00:13:28,724 --> 00:13:29,685
[SPEAKER_01]: And you just obscure it.

155
00:13:30,525 --> 00:13:55,343
[SPEAKER_01]: When you read in the suttas, when the Buddha would encourage people to investigate, well, not investigate, but really contemplate the various parts of your body, as in a basis for experiencing a non-attraction and revulsion, that's basically to undermine your gratuitous notion of body is beautiful, or to undermine your gratuitous notion of you are in control, like you are the master of this body.

156
00:13:56,686 --> 00:14:13,332
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you... in order to practice that contemplation rightly, you need to do it on the basis of that body that has arisen, on the basis of that ownership of your body, which means you need to start seeing these parts of the body within that single unit of the functional arrangement that your body is.

157
00:14:14,872 --> 00:14:22,975
[SPEAKER_01]: Because by seeing that there are parts of this functional arrangement of unit of the body that I own, you can see that actually that ownership is gratuitous.

158
00:14:23,335 --> 00:14:24,676
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not that that body is not real.

159
00:14:26,515 --> 00:14:35,532
[SPEAKER_01]: The ownership is gratuitous because this thing that I own, that I think I'm the full master controller, is fully determined by these random things that I can't even access.

160
00:14:36,494 --> 00:14:38,478
[SPEAKER_01]: For example, your beating heart.

161
00:14:40,001 --> 00:14:42,383
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I see even the expression, my beating heart.

162
00:14:42,923 --> 00:14:46,065
[SPEAKER_01]: You kind of assume the ownership because it belongs to this body that you own.

163
00:14:46,645 --> 00:14:52,549
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you trying to say, oh, well, see, this heart is on its own, operates on its own, if it stops, my life is gone.

164
00:14:52,569 --> 00:14:53,209
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all true.

165
00:14:54,110 --> 00:15:01,135
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you use that argument to say that, thus, there is really no body there, it's just these parts, well, that's just obscuring the issue.

166
00:15:02,135 --> 00:15:07,699
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there is the body there, because otherwise, if there is nothing there, how can you even direct your denial at it?

167
00:15:09,035 --> 00:15:11,059
[SPEAKER_01]: It has to be there to some extent.

168
00:15:11,119 --> 00:15:21,399
[SPEAKER_01]: And that extent is the functional arrangement of the body that is actually comprised out of individual independent parts, not me and my sense of self and my sense of mastery and my control.

169
00:15:22,951 --> 00:15:46,265
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, it's mentioned in the suttas often, this kind of reflection of various bodily parts, but it needs to be done correctly, needs to be done on the basis of the body, so that then you can remove that which is not necessary in regard to that experience, and that is your own ownership of it, your gratuitous assumption of mastery of your life, like fundamentally, even like biologically, my own heart.

170
00:15:47,532 --> 00:15:51,313
[SPEAKER_01]: But if it were yours, you would be able to tell it when to beat and how to beat.

171
00:15:51,953 --> 00:15:54,694
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't be completely left out, pushed outside of it.

172
00:15:54,734 --> 00:15:58,655
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't even access the notion of voluntarily controlling your heart.

173
00:15:59,355 --> 00:16:03,476
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, if that random pumping muscle in your chest stops, your life's gone.

174
00:16:03,996 --> 00:16:04,596
[SPEAKER_01]: That instant.

175
00:16:05,556 --> 00:16:09,277
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, at the same time, you maintain the full notion of mastery over your life.

176
00:16:11,497 --> 00:16:17,402
[SPEAKER_01]: So, looking at the heart is just... if done correctly, will approve that gratuitous mastery.

177
00:16:17,743 --> 00:16:20,846
[SPEAKER_01]: It will not deny the reality of your life.

178
00:16:21,723 --> 00:16:26,266
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why Arahant, who is fully uprooted, an enlightened person, is fully uprooted.

179
00:16:26,627 --> 00:16:29,989
[SPEAKER_01]: He still operates normally, seemingly, from external point of view.

180
00:16:30,410 --> 00:16:34,072
[SPEAKER_01]: He has intentions, he has choices, he has decisions.

181
00:16:34,212 --> 00:16:34,993
[SPEAKER_01]: All that stays.

182
00:16:35,533 --> 00:16:37,955
[SPEAKER_01]: What he doesn't have is the ownership of the five aggregates.

183
00:16:37,975 --> 00:16:41,238
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't have the ownership of the experience as a whole.

184
00:16:42,395 --> 00:16:44,516
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't own his body, he doesn't own his intentions.

185
00:16:45,016 --> 00:16:50,259
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't mean he's... And the only way to arrive, basically, at that point of non-ownership, is to first accept it.

186
00:16:50,780 --> 00:17:02,006
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, the accepting of responsibility for your own actions, accepting the validity, basically, of actions, of the results of actions, of the results of practice, needs to be the first step.

187
00:17:02,666 --> 00:17:03,927
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the mundane right view.

188
00:17:04,427 --> 00:17:08,930
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you keep denying it, as it's not real, you're denying the basis for your practice.

189
00:17:19,148 --> 00:17:23,712
[SPEAKER_00]: It's very easy to deny the problem.

190
00:17:23,732 --> 00:17:25,353
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no self.

191
00:17:25,513 --> 00:17:31,417
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the most natural reaction, which is bound to the most fundamental attitude that the Buddha was describing.

192
00:17:31,938 --> 00:17:34,119
[SPEAKER_01]: When there is a pleasure, affirm it further.

193
00:17:34,760 --> 00:17:36,701
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how people react.

194
00:17:36,761 --> 00:17:39,103
[SPEAKER_01]: When people experience the unpleasant attitude, deny it.

195
00:17:39,644 --> 00:17:40,724
[SPEAKER_01]: Try to get rid of it.

196
00:17:40,784 --> 00:17:41,885
[SPEAKER_01]: Remove it.

197
00:17:41,945 --> 00:17:45,168
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's like craving in regard to pleasant, craving in regard to unpleasant.

198
00:17:48,836 --> 00:17:54,337
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in this particular instance, you realize that there is a problem.

199
00:17:54,357 --> 00:18:02,419
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a problem of the gratuitous sense of self, that thinks it's a master and owner, yet the experience keeps contradicting it.

200
00:18:03,259 --> 00:18:11,881
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the suffering, the discrepancy of experience in suffering, should be enough for an authentic transparent person to see that actually you are not in control.

201
00:18:13,425 --> 00:18:18,148
[SPEAKER_01]: And your gratuitous notion of control, it's the reason for your suffering.

202
00:18:18,248 --> 00:18:22,991
[SPEAKER_01]: You are the one responsible for the discrepancy of your existence that then results in suffering.

203
00:18:24,032 --> 00:18:28,655
[SPEAKER_01]: Or results in liab... in, in, sorry, in, yeah, in being liable to suffering.

204
00:18:28,735 --> 00:18:30,656
[SPEAKER_01]: It results in possibility to suffer.

205
00:18:31,276 --> 00:18:33,278
[SPEAKER_01]: Possibility to be affected by suffering.

206
00:18:33,938 --> 00:18:36,600
[SPEAKER_01]: And that in itself, recognition of that, is what suffering is.

207
00:18:43,743 --> 00:18:46,844
[SPEAKER_00]: What about seeing yourself directly?

208
00:18:46,864 --> 00:18:47,184
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry?

209
00:18:47,364 --> 00:18:50,084
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeing yourself directly?

210
00:18:50,304 --> 00:18:51,604
[SPEAKER_01]: Seeing your sense of self directly?

211
00:18:51,624 --> 00:18:52,124
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you can't.

212
00:18:52,605 --> 00:18:54,325
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you'll be seeing it with your sense of self.

213
00:18:54,445 --> 00:18:58,586
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, you'll be appropriating that in that gratuitous sense of mastery over this sense of self.

214
00:19:00,166 --> 00:19:03,927
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, the sense of self, it's always... That's what I mean, that's why I said, it's a symptom.

215
00:19:04,667 --> 00:19:07,327
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not an issue that you can see and then directly address.

216
00:19:08,227 --> 00:19:09,428
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a symptom caused by...

217
00:19:09,468 --> 00:19:11,848
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you... you have to start looking at it as such.

218
00:19:13,167 --> 00:19:21,297
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you blame the symptom for the illness, you're not gonna see the illness that undermines it, that underlies it, sorry.

219
00:19:23,140 --> 00:19:28,546
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to recognize that you can't access that sense of self directly, because it's done with the notion of self.

220
00:19:29,272 --> 00:19:37,055
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means your senses... you might think, oh, this is myself, and that means that your... that gratuitous sense of ownership in regard to that experience, is in the background of it.

221
00:19:37,395 --> 00:19:39,056
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always elsewhere from where you look.

222
00:19:39,556 --> 00:19:45,459
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to stop trying to expect you're gonna find it through, like, looking and spotting where it is.

223
00:19:46,219 --> 00:19:48,680
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you don't need to.

224
00:19:48,880 --> 00:19:50,781
[SPEAKER_01]: Once you recognize it's a symptom, okay.

225
00:19:51,141 --> 00:19:52,882
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you stop trying to chase the symptom.

226
00:19:53,471 --> 00:19:57,275
[SPEAKER_01]: You use it as a criteria whether the illness is still present.

227
00:19:58,115 --> 00:20:13,670
[SPEAKER_01]: And that illness is the sense of ownership of things that you do have say in, that you can kind of experience and relate to directly, such as I choose sensuality, I choose ill-will, I choose this over this because I'm purely acting out of desire for pleasure.

228
00:20:14,787 --> 00:20:17,108
[SPEAKER_01]: Desire and lust in regard to the five aggregates.

229
00:20:17,168 --> 00:20:18,088
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what the Guru described.

230
00:20:18,348 --> 00:20:26,250
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the upādāna and the ultimate upādāna, the ultimate attachment, is the attachment of the self, self-view.

231
00:20:26,750 --> 00:20:28,070
[SPEAKER_01]: That's just a tea-picking lady.

232
00:20:28,391 --> 00:20:30,071
[SPEAKER_01]: Stop barking.

233
00:20:31,051 --> 00:20:34,532
[SPEAKER_01]: The ultimate, the ultimate upādāna is the upādāna of the self-view.

234
00:20:35,672 --> 00:20:41,314
[SPEAKER_01]: And so you maintain basically that symptom, as in, because you keep engaging in that which is keeping you sick.

235
00:20:42,780 --> 00:20:45,741
[SPEAKER_01]: You keep maintaining your desire and lust in your experience.

236
00:20:45,821 --> 00:20:47,601
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's something that you knowingly do.

237
00:20:49,942 --> 00:20:52,862
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the problem with people who kind of are familiar with the Buddhist practice.

238
00:20:53,702 --> 00:20:55,183
[SPEAKER_01]: They're aware of the sense of self.

239
00:20:55,363 --> 00:20:58,904
[SPEAKER_01]: They're aware that it's supposed to be seen as not myself, as in anatta.

240
00:20:59,684 --> 00:21:01,264
[SPEAKER_01]: But they don't see necessarily the connection.

241
00:21:01,284 --> 00:21:03,525
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then they just keep trying to deny the sense of self.

242
00:21:03,945 --> 00:21:06,545
[SPEAKER_01]: Trying to, like, oh, not mine, not mine, not mine, not mine.

243
00:21:06,966 --> 00:21:08,766
[SPEAKER_01]: And address it on its own terms.

244
00:21:09,878 --> 00:21:15,444
[SPEAKER_01]: Failing to see, basically, that it's their desire and lust, they're fully aware in regard to other things throughout their life.

245
00:21:16,005 --> 00:21:17,547
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the connection that they're missing.

246
00:21:19,509 --> 00:21:25,336
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why, because of that, the whole... the sense of... sense restraint and sīla and virtue comes first.

247
00:21:26,854 --> 00:21:35,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's... you can't possibly not be perpetuating desire and lust in your thought, when your physical actions and verbal actions are fully engaged in it.

248
00:21:36,497 --> 00:21:43,499
[SPEAKER_01]: You can think your thoughts are pure as much as you want, but they're not, because your behavior is still perpetuating that mistaken pattern.

249
00:21:46,360 --> 00:21:54,102
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to start restraining it on that level, so that then it can... it can become clearly visible on the level of your thought.

250
00:22:00,205 --> 00:22:01,226
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

251
00:22:01,266 --> 00:22:18,478
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to see the connection between these things, as in your gratuitous sense of ownership is maintained through your careless giving in to the desire, giving in to the ill-will, giving in to the distraction, giving in to the unwholesome states in your daily experience.

252
00:22:18,779 --> 00:22:19,799
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's where the practice is.

253
00:22:20,240 --> 00:22:25,083
[SPEAKER_01]: Being watchful of those things, not being watchful of everything and nothing, or trying to catch your sense of self,

254
00:22:25,410 --> 00:22:28,512
[SPEAKER_01]: We're trying to catch a perfect moment that will reveal that nothing is real.

255
00:22:28,552 --> 00:22:30,053
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, there is no self really.

256
00:22:30,473 --> 00:22:30,593
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

257
00:22:30,973 --> 00:22:33,915
[SPEAKER_01]: None of that was ever a problem.

258
00:22:35,396 --> 00:22:37,537
[SPEAKER_01]: The only problem is you engaging in unwholesome.

259
00:22:37,637 --> 00:22:39,598
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it's defined as unwholesome.

260
00:22:40,419 --> 00:22:50,345
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it keeps... it's a direct determination of the problem of suffering, of the problem of gratuitous ownership and mastery of your experience.

261
00:22:51,786 --> 00:22:52,887
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's unwholesome.

262
00:22:53,347 --> 00:22:55,648
[SPEAKER_01]: If it doesn't partake in that, it's not unwholesome.

263
00:22:55,708 --> 00:22:57,749
[SPEAKER_01]: It means it's either neutral or it's either wholesome.

264
00:23:03,352 --> 00:23:05,414
[SPEAKER_01]: Hey!

265
00:23:06,994 --> 00:23:07,315
[SPEAKER_01]: Shut it!

266
00:23:08,635 --> 00:23:09,416
[SPEAKER_01]: Shut it!

267
00:23:12,037 --> 00:23:12,718
[SPEAKER_01]: Go and play with Jabu.

268
00:23:12,958 --> 00:23:13,338
[SPEAKER_01]: He's healthy.

269
00:23:29,137 --> 00:23:35,819
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what happens then, when you no longer own that sense of self?

270
00:23:37,419 --> 00:23:39,000
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's the true definition of freedom.

271
00:23:39,560 --> 00:23:41,341
[SPEAKER_01]: You have basically laid down the burden.

272
00:23:43,741 --> 00:23:48,603
[SPEAKER_01]: The burden of action, the burden of... You have laid down the liability to suffer.

273
00:23:49,563 --> 00:23:50,583
[SPEAKER_01]: It just cannot affect you.

274
00:23:52,564 --> 00:23:54,164
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the direct measure of that freedom.

275
00:23:54,524 --> 00:23:56,385
[SPEAKER_01]: Inability to suffer amidst suffering.

276
00:23:59,771 --> 00:24:01,392
[SPEAKER_00]: It sounds like a kind of madness.

277
00:24:02,292 --> 00:24:04,333
[SPEAKER_00]: Like somebody who denies...

278
00:24:04,753 --> 00:24:04,974
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

279
00:24:05,374 --> 00:24:05,734
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, no.

280
00:24:06,094 --> 00:24:07,075
[SPEAKER_01]: See, that's what I mean.

281
00:24:07,095 --> 00:24:11,957
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I keep saying, you have to start with non-denial, because it will result exactly in that.

282
00:24:12,337 --> 00:24:19,981
[SPEAKER_01]: People doing all sorts of horrendous things and then saying, well, there is really nobody there, which was exactly one of the wrong views that the Buddha was criticizing.

283
00:24:21,021 --> 00:24:31,364
[SPEAKER_01]: Back in the day, when in India, they believed... and actually, I read that, that was in Bhagavad Gita, was the whole point of saying nobody... you can go and kill, you can chop people's heads off, and nobody gets killed.

284
00:24:31,824 --> 00:24:34,545
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just the sword running through the elements and whatnot.

285
00:24:35,065 --> 00:24:37,485
[SPEAKER_01]: And the Buddha criticized that as extremely wrong view.

286
00:24:37,946 --> 00:24:40,706
[SPEAKER_01]: They can result in a lot of suffering for you, if you act out of it.

287
00:24:41,707 --> 00:24:47,048
[SPEAKER_01]: See, that's when you basically jump to a conclusion, not develop the understanding of the conclusion.

288
00:24:48,317 --> 00:24:53,661
[SPEAKER_01]: And, if you actually look closer at this conclusion, you realize, okay, in order to understand it, I must stop denying things.

289
00:24:54,461 --> 00:24:57,183
[SPEAKER_01]: I must accept that human life is a real thing.

290
00:24:57,203 --> 00:24:58,624
[SPEAKER_01]: A choice is a real thing.

291
00:24:58,644 --> 00:25:00,766
[SPEAKER_01]: The body, it's a reality as such.

292
00:25:01,707 --> 00:25:05,589
[SPEAKER_01]: Things appear differently, but they're real in whatever manner they appear.

293
00:25:06,370 --> 00:25:10,913
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even a pure abstract thought, it is real as an abstract thought.

294
00:25:11,213 --> 00:25:14,055
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a definition of phenomenology.

295
00:25:14,115 --> 00:25:17,278
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, basically, understanding of phenomena, understanding of appearance.

296
00:25:18,177 --> 00:25:19,899
[SPEAKER_01]: But people get a hint of it, maybe.

297
00:25:20,120 --> 00:25:21,301
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, there is really no self.

298
00:25:21,621 --> 00:25:24,405
[SPEAKER_01]: They find that view pleasing, without any understanding of it.

299
00:25:24,685 --> 00:25:26,267
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's when they develop these conclusions.

300
00:25:26,367 --> 00:25:30,012
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, so, I can do whatever I want, because there's nobody... there is no doer there.

301
00:25:30,453 --> 00:25:32,415
[SPEAKER_01]: And there is nobody who receives that doing either.

302
00:25:32,435 --> 00:25:33,437
[SPEAKER_01]: There is nobody affected.

303
00:25:33,997 --> 00:25:44,503
[SPEAKER_01]: And that was exactly the view when the... in the time of the Buddha, and before, and obviously after, whereby you can say things such as, no, no, no, it's your duty to go and kill these people.

304
00:25:44,904 --> 00:25:48,386
[SPEAKER_01]: You can go and kill them, and nobody gets killed anyway.

305
00:25:48,426 --> 00:25:50,847
[SPEAKER_01]: We are all already divine and saved and whatnot.

306
00:25:53,229 --> 00:25:53,909
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

307
00:25:53,929 --> 00:25:59,413
[SPEAKER_01]: The denial must stop first, in order for basis of understanding to arise.

308
00:26:01,214 --> 00:26:03,035
[SPEAKER_01]: Because how can you understand the ownership?

309
00:26:03,863 --> 00:26:08,506
[SPEAKER_01]: of something that you appropriate as yours, if you keep denying that you appropriated anything.

310
00:26:11,647 --> 00:26:15,409
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, when are you gonna give me back, eh, I don't know, those books I lend you?

311
00:26:15,629 --> 00:26:16,010
[SPEAKER_01]: What books?

312
00:26:16,170 --> 00:26:16,710
[SPEAKER_01]: There are no books.

313
00:26:17,550 --> 00:26:18,271
[SPEAKER_01]: Books don't exist.

314
00:26:18,611 --> 00:26:19,391
[SPEAKER_01]: What are you talking about?

315
00:26:21,352 --> 00:26:21,973
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you took it.

316
00:26:23,193 --> 00:26:26,515
[SPEAKER_01]: And in this case, it's even worse, because you keep taking it, that which is not yours.

317
00:26:26,535 --> 00:26:27,776
[SPEAKER_01]: You keep regarding it as mine.

318
00:26:32,463 --> 00:26:34,964
[SPEAKER_00]: So, who's the real... who's the real owner?

319
00:26:35,664 --> 00:26:41,627
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the real... the question of a real owner is based on the already that misperceived owner that you are.

320
00:26:42,487 --> 00:26:44,648
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, okay, so if I'm not the owner, who's the real owner?

321
00:26:44,948 --> 00:26:51,371
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly, again, another view back in India, that's very prevalent today, the greater sense of self.

322
00:26:51,491 --> 00:26:54,772
[SPEAKER_01]: You abandon your sense of self by surrendering it to the greater sense of self.

323
00:26:55,232 --> 00:27:01,895
[SPEAKER_01]: But the slightest notion of the sense of self, you can only relate to it because you're maintaining it on your own self level.

324
00:27:02,831 --> 00:27:19,937
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, if you abandon your sense of self, truly, if you see it as thoroughly unownable, so to speak, thoroughly independent of you, because it depends upon things that you cannot even access, let alone control and tell him what to do, notion, any extension of that sense of self is seen under the same nature.

325
00:27:20,577 --> 00:27:22,098
[SPEAKER_01]: Not mine, not for me, not myself.

326
00:27:23,088 --> 00:27:31,858
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if people can conceive and conceive and act out of the greater sense of self, as in God, for example, eh... they do so based on their very personal sense of self.

327
00:27:32,658 --> 00:27:38,685
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why... that's why Sartre meant in... in... that basically the... the most common idea of God is just the extension.

328
00:27:38,745 --> 00:27:43,830
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically... it's the ideal of your sense of self, being the ultimate master creator.

329
00:27:44,511 --> 00:27:51,633
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you project into this outer universe of the great, the great God, being of a God, or whatever, the mystic energy, and so on.

330
00:27:52,373 --> 00:27:56,253
[SPEAKER_01]: All of that is, is relatable only through your sense of self.

331
00:27:57,534 --> 00:27:58,334
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's another thing.

332
00:27:58,594 --> 00:28:12,897
[SPEAKER_01]: You can either deny your sense of self, try to get rid of it, which fails, or you can try to envelope it in a greater sense of self, which is just, again, just extension, proliferation of it, which is even worse, because it's another way of obscuring your own sense of self.

333
00:28:17,274 --> 00:28:17,874
[SPEAKER_01]: Same.

334
00:28:17,934 --> 00:28:20,136
[SPEAKER_01]: It was God's will and so on.

335
00:28:20,336 --> 00:28:21,116
[SPEAKER_01]: You decided.

336
00:28:22,237 --> 00:28:29,781
[SPEAKER_01]: You decided that what you've done was actually rooted in the greater sense of self because, yeah, truly, intentions arise on their own.

337
00:28:30,602 --> 00:28:32,623
[SPEAKER_01]: But you're the one who chooses to act upon them.

338
00:28:33,902 --> 00:28:35,243
[SPEAKER_01]: Intentions present themselves.

339
00:28:36,063 --> 00:28:41,425
[SPEAKER_01]: You see a cup, you can use it for drinking, you can use it for throwing, chasing a... chasing... chasing...

340
00:28:41,805 --> 00:28:43,226
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know... chasing a wild animal.

341
00:28:43,546 --> 00:28:45,347
[SPEAKER_01]: You can use it for all these sorts of things.

342
00:28:45,467 --> 00:28:48,848
[SPEAKER_01]: It's an object that has all this intentionality implied in it.

343
00:28:49,429 --> 00:28:54,651
[SPEAKER_01]: But you choose to use it in a certain manner on account of whatever you're trying to achieve.

344
00:28:55,678 --> 00:29:05,841
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, dismissing the... basically dismissing the reality of the cup, or the object you chose to use, you hope to cover up your responsibility for the choice of that thing.

345
00:29:07,221 --> 00:29:08,262
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's something you can't do.

346
00:29:08,722 --> 00:29:19,285
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, ascribing the intentionality of an object to a greater God, and then saying, well, see, these things were presented to me in all this manner, that's why I used it.

347
00:29:19,385 --> 00:29:20,105
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not my fault.

348
00:29:21,163 --> 00:29:31,107
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's not your fault that things were presented in a manner of options, but it is your fault to choose to act upon it irresponsibly and deny the responsibility or act out of greed, aversion, delusion.

349
00:29:31,907 --> 00:29:32,407
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what I mean.

350
00:29:32,467 --> 00:29:33,328
[SPEAKER_01]: Denial must stop.

351
00:29:35,108 --> 00:29:38,810
[SPEAKER_01]: Because as long as you deny things, you interfere with that which is actually not on you.

352
00:29:39,510 --> 00:29:42,611
[SPEAKER_01]: Arising of the thoughts, arising of the options, arising of the intentions.

353
00:29:43,131 --> 00:29:47,973
[SPEAKER_01]: But you then choosing to act in a certain manner on account of this or on account of that, that's on you.

354
00:29:49,172 --> 00:29:57,835
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why the Buddha would say, all he teaches, basically, is suffering and freedom from it, in a sense of, by avoiding unwholesome, cultivate the wholesome.

355
00:30:00,356 --> 00:30:05,158
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's not unwholesome to have a thought of throwing this object at somebody.

356
00:30:06,894 --> 00:30:14,356
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you then start relishing that thought and thinking upon it further intentionally, now that bit is unwholesome and that's on you.

357
00:30:15,196 --> 00:30:26,458
[SPEAKER_01]: Or even worse, if you choose then to verbally start acting upon it in an abusive manner and then physically start abusing somebody, well, that's of course, that's just a further proliferation of that unwholesome attitude.

358
00:30:28,098 --> 00:30:28,658
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

359
00:30:28,678 --> 00:30:30,659
[SPEAKER_01]: The responsibility must be taken first.

360
00:30:31,539 --> 00:30:35,820
[SPEAKER_01]: The denial of things must be... must be basically abandoned.

361
00:30:36,915 --> 00:30:39,716
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you created the basis for the practice of dhamma.

362
00:30:39,896 --> 00:30:40,916
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why it's a middle way.

363
00:30:41,656 --> 00:30:43,956
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you don't go and deny everything or affirm everything.

364
00:30:44,477 --> 00:30:48,857
[SPEAKER_01]: You recognize where the line is, where your responsibility begins, and where it ends.

365
00:30:50,378 --> 00:30:53,318
[SPEAKER_01]: And you start thinking like that, you can make sense out of what arahant is.

366
00:30:54,499 --> 00:31:01,180
[SPEAKER_01]: Because people were often like, what, arahant is this, like, thoughtless, basically robot, who just acts automatically.

367
00:31:01,997 --> 00:31:02,137
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

368
00:31:02,697 --> 00:31:13,303
[SPEAKER_01]: He's just a person who has basically removed any ownership, any appropriation, any interference of things that arise naturally, which means he's incapable of acting unwholesomely.

369
00:31:14,223 --> 00:31:16,985
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't mean that whatever he does or says will be pleasing to people.

370
00:31:18,505 --> 00:31:21,187
[SPEAKER_01]: It means that he cannot be rooted in greed, aversion, delusion.

371
00:31:21,967 --> 00:31:23,288
[SPEAKER_01]: Cannot be rooted in a wrong view.

372
00:31:37,037 --> 00:31:37,537
[SPEAKER_01]: Come here, boy.

373
00:31:40,560 --> 00:31:41,021
[SPEAKER_01]: Come here.

374
00:31:42,462 --> 00:31:42,982
[SPEAKER_01]: Come here, boy.

375
00:31:43,243 --> 00:31:44,544
[SPEAKER_01]: Eh, Jabu.

376
00:31:46,726 --> 00:31:49,028
[SPEAKER_01]: Eh, eh, eh, eh, eh.

377
00:31:49,368 --> 00:31:49,728
[SPEAKER_01]: Focus.

378
00:31:50,029 --> 00:31:50,269
[SPEAKER_01]: Sit.

379
00:31:52,451 --> 00:31:52,871
[SPEAKER_00]: Good boy.

380
00:31:52,951 --> 00:31:53,872
[SPEAKER_01]: Come here.

381
00:31:53,992 --> 00:31:54,373
[SPEAKER_01]: Come close.

382
00:31:54,693 --> 00:31:55,073
[SPEAKER_01]: Come close.

383
00:31:55,714 --> 00:31:56,194
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, oh.

384
00:31:57,175 --> 00:31:57,896
[SPEAKER_01]: You're a Jabu.

385
00:31:58,957 --> 00:32:00,118
[SPEAKER_01]: But you're a good girl.

386
00:32:01,442 --> 00:32:02,384
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't want it to go down there.

387
00:32:02,404 --> 00:32:04,066
[SPEAKER_01]: That fence needs to be fixed.

388
00:32:04,086 --> 00:32:04,707
[SPEAKER_01]: You might discover.

389
00:32:04,868 --> 00:32:07,873
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, you rude!

390
00:32:36,589 --> 00:32:54,399
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, in practical terms, and kind of, ultimately, all a person would need to do, really, is, eh... keep an eye on their own intentions, motivations, what makes them act.

391
00:32:55,179 --> 00:33:02,364
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's what determines wholesome and unwholesome, and then how wholesome or how unwholesome it can be.

392
00:33:04,490 --> 00:33:08,132
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's something you cannot not be aware of.

393
00:33:08,273 --> 00:33:12,515
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, mindfulness is the... is something you can't have too much of, as the Buddha would describe.

394
00:33:13,816 --> 00:33:17,539
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like a rigid, mechanical observation of moment to moment to moment.

395
00:33:17,579 --> 00:33:17,759
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

396
00:33:17,879 --> 00:33:21,101
[SPEAKER_01]: It's awareness of that which is already present.

397
00:33:21,161 --> 00:33:22,602
[SPEAKER_01]: Fundamentally, intentions.

398
00:33:25,284 --> 00:33:31,428
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you're mindful basically of the... of your body being... you're mindful of your situation that's already intentional.

399
00:33:32,307 --> 00:33:35,828
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're mindful of your feelings, you're mindful of the situation that's already intentional.

400
00:33:36,228 --> 00:33:37,988
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, you're then mindful.

401
00:33:39,569 --> 00:33:41,829
[SPEAKER_01]: Not, not necessarily to the full extent.

402
00:33:42,349 --> 00:33:46,771
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha said, mindfulness developed to the extent necessary for the final knowledge.

403
00:33:47,831 --> 00:33:52,492
[SPEAKER_01]: But, the possibility of you being mindful of it is always there, because these things are present.

404
00:33:53,798 --> 00:34:00,567
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that means you can actually then discern the background of whatever your situation is, which is already... that background is part of it.

405
00:34:01,208 --> 00:34:04,752
[SPEAKER_01]: You can then discern the subtler motivation behind your actions.

406
00:34:05,553 --> 00:34:09,058
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you discern it, you will see if it's wholesome or unwholesome.

407
00:34:09,685 --> 00:34:13,948
[SPEAKER_01]: Is this rooted in desire, sensuality, greed, aversion?

408
00:34:14,068 --> 00:34:17,410
[SPEAKER_01]: Is this rooted really in your will, desire to harm or something?

409
00:34:17,890 --> 00:34:24,374
[SPEAKER_01]: Or is genuinely rooted in good, in non-greed, non-aversion?

410
00:34:24,514 --> 00:34:28,616
[SPEAKER_01]: And if... once you discern it, you'll be able to then not act out of it.

411
00:34:28,676 --> 00:34:29,617
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's all you need to do.

412
00:34:30,438 --> 00:34:34,400
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to try to get rid of the possibilities of unwholesome.

413
00:34:35,521 --> 00:34:37,242
[SPEAKER_01]: They're just part of how things arise.

414
00:34:38,109 --> 00:34:40,030
[SPEAKER_01]: You can use it for good or for bad.

415
00:34:40,831 --> 00:34:46,834
[SPEAKER_01]: Responsibility is on your choice how to use it, not on the possibility of how it can be used.

416
00:34:48,915 --> 00:35:05,825
[SPEAKER_01]: But none of that can become apparent if you're just sort of completely... if you have completely obscured that responsibility of your own choice, that wholesome, that unwholesome, and you obscure it through denying or through projecting it onto someone else, someone else's fault, it's the God's will and everything else.

417
00:35:07,461 --> 00:35:13,499
[SPEAKER_01]: Say, even if it's God's will to present you with a certain choice, He can't make you choose this A over B.

418
00:35:15,248 --> 00:35:23,372
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the early existential philosophers said, it's not that the phenomenology and phenomenological method, it's atheistic or anything like that.

419
00:35:23,972 --> 00:35:27,633
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically just recognizing the fact that God has no power over your choice.

420
00:35:28,434 --> 00:35:34,296
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can allow the gods to exist as much as you want, but none of the gods can tell you how to choose it.

421
00:35:34,536 --> 00:35:37,258
[SPEAKER_01]: You are the one who chooses what God told you.

422
00:35:38,038 --> 00:35:38,759
[SPEAKER_01]: To act upon it.

423
00:35:39,419 --> 00:35:53,751
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, whether things come your way out of complete circumstance, randomness, accident, God's will, previous action, previous karma and vipaka and whatever else, makes no difference, because you presently are responsible for choosing how to act upon it.

424
00:35:56,153 --> 00:35:58,575
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, this is because of... No.

425
00:35:58,595 --> 00:35:58,835
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry.

426
00:35:59,415 --> 00:36:00,736
[SPEAKER_01]: I've done this because of that.

427
00:36:01,197 --> 00:36:01,397
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

428
00:36:01,577 --> 00:36:05,080
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you've done that, because you chose this to be the reason for it.

429
00:36:10,409 --> 00:36:13,311
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you can always say no to everything, or yes to everything.

430
00:36:14,812 --> 00:36:18,915
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, you can always do the opposite of whatever is presented to you.

431
00:36:20,116 --> 00:36:21,377
[SPEAKER_01]: Means the choice is always on you.

432
00:36:21,998 --> 00:36:28,563
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the whole definition in the philosophy was basically, man is condemned to be free.

433
00:36:29,858 --> 00:36:31,379
[SPEAKER_01]: But you cannot not choose.

434
00:36:32,479 --> 00:36:35,341
[SPEAKER_01]: And sometimes people think, if I abstain from choice, I didn't choose.

435
00:36:35,761 --> 00:36:37,842
[SPEAKER_01]: But abstaining from choice, it's another choice.

436
00:36:38,263 --> 00:36:39,203
[SPEAKER_01]: You just chose that.

437
00:36:39,904 --> 00:36:43,406
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why the Buddha said, the being... means exactly this, basically.

438
00:36:43,866 --> 00:36:45,627
[SPEAKER_01]: Beings are bound with their actions.

439
00:36:45,947 --> 00:36:46,987
[SPEAKER_01]: They cannot escape it.

440
00:36:47,948 --> 00:36:51,450
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, abstaining from your action becomes an action for you.

441
00:36:52,805 --> 00:37:02,253
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only way to escape the action is to truly first accept it, and then see the degree of your responsibility in it, and stop maintaining that.

442
00:37:02,773 --> 00:37:11,740
[SPEAKER_01]: Not trying to overly deny action or overly explain it.

443
00:37:12,421 --> 00:37:17,445
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in practical terms,

444
00:37:18,753 --> 00:37:30,957
[SPEAKER_01]: If people want to meditate and practice correctly, in this manner, always keep a corner of your eye, at the back of your mind, basically, where your intentions are.

445
00:37:31,717 --> 00:37:34,758
[SPEAKER_01]: And before you do something, check with yourself, why are you doing it?

446
00:37:35,158 --> 00:37:36,259
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do you want to do it?

447
00:37:36,279 --> 00:37:37,959
[SPEAKER_00]: You always get an answer.

448
00:37:38,739 --> 00:37:39,260
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

449
00:37:39,340 --> 00:37:44,101
[SPEAKER_01]: It might take a time to cut through all that, like layers of cover-ups, but it's there.

450
00:37:44,341 --> 00:37:44,921
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you know the truth.

451
00:37:45,342 --> 00:37:46,362
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you know the truth.

452
00:37:49,073 --> 00:38:01,279
[SPEAKER_01]: As we were discussing before, the simile, I was telling you... well, the example, it wasn't really the simile, but... like, say... say you're somebody who is on a... on a... on a... oh, that's a beautiful eagle.

453
00:38:03,640 --> 00:38:16,427
[SPEAKER_01]: You're somebody who is on a... on a, like a... not necessarily a strict meditation retreat, but, say, you're practicing seclusion, practicing non-distraction and so on, and then you have a thought that arises, oh, I want to go...

454
00:38:16,447 --> 00:38:18,428
[SPEAKER_01]: I want to go to the... to the library.

455
00:38:21,772 --> 00:38:25,055
[SPEAKER_01]: And now, to get some sutta books, so I can read and study harder.

456
00:38:25,695 --> 00:38:31,000
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you see, on the external, based on the external point of view, that's a good thing to do.

457
00:38:31,661 --> 00:38:35,984
[SPEAKER_01]: Reading suttas, enriching the basis of your knowledge, contemplation, and so on.

458
00:38:36,625 --> 00:38:38,687
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not the point of view that matters.

459
00:38:39,307 --> 00:38:41,890
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the internal point of view that matters for whoever practices it.

460
00:38:42,746 --> 00:38:50,197
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what you should want to do, not like try to justify your impending action based on the external acceptance of it or denial of it.

461
00:38:50,557 --> 00:38:53,802
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to justify it or not based on the internal reasoning.

462
00:38:54,443 --> 00:38:59,530
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you start looking at it, why do I really want to go to the library and dig these books right now?

463
00:39:01,983 --> 00:39:11,090
[SPEAKER_01]: Wait for a while, because probably you're not going to get the answer immediately, but you can then see that, oh, actually, I want to go, because I hope there'll be some people there, and I'll end up being distracted.

464
00:39:11,691 --> 00:39:13,692
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll end up talking to somebody, because I feel a bit...

465
00:39:14,558 --> 00:39:16,939
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, pressure of doing nothing, being alone.

466
00:39:17,520 --> 00:39:21,421
[SPEAKER_01]: So, going into into the group of people can alleviate that pressure.

467
00:39:22,082 --> 00:39:24,343
[SPEAKER_01]: But I can't admit that to myself directly.

468
00:39:24,403 --> 00:39:32,787
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I find an excuse of going to library to take the suttas, where I know there is a 90% of chance there will be like-minded people and they're gonna start talking to me.

469
00:39:33,407 --> 00:39:34,908
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's the whole point.

470
00:39:34,928 --> 00:39:39,691
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, I won't feel responsible for choosing to distract myself, because, well, they started talking to me.

471
00:39:40,271 --> 00:39:42,392
[SPEAKER_01]: It would have been rude to just remain silent.

472
00:39:43,813 --> 00:39:58,396
[SPEAKER_01]: But you fail to see that you already decided to talk to them by making yourself liable to that possibility of going in the environment where there's a good likelihood of people distracting you from your own, basically, self-awareness and solitude and so on.

473
00:40:00,607 --> 00:40:02,689
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now you can say, oh, so I'll never go to library again.

474
00:40:03,190 --> 00:40:04,331
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's also impossible.

475
00:40:04,371 --> 00:40:06,173
[SPEAKER_01]: That's also trying to... overdoing it.

476
00:40:07,034 --> 00:40:07,174
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

477
00:40:07,615 --> 00:40:09,657
[SPEAKER_01]: Go to the library, but for the right reasons.

478
00:40:09,797 --> 00:40:17,886
[SPEAKER_01]: There might be a genuine need to go to library, but it was the unwholesome part of your mind that made it into the unwholesome, rooted it in unwholesome desire.

479
00:40:18,978 --> 00:40:21,381
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yes, certain things you can abandon straight away.

480
00:40:21,622 --> 00:40:24,686
[SPEAKER_01]: Engagement in sensuality, sexuality, lying, stealing.

481
00:40:24,706 --> 00:40:25,387
[SPEAKER_01]: You abandon that.

482
00:40:25,407 --> 00:40:26,889
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no wholesome way of doing that.

483
00:40:27,570 --> 00:40:30,114
[SPEAKER_01]: But as the Buddha pointed out, there are things such as eating food.

484
00:40:31,075 --> 00:40:32,738
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't stop that, because you die.

485
00:40:34,658 --> 00:40:52,261
[SPEAKER_01]: But, if you eat it carelessly, if you're not aware of your reasons behind eating the food, if you don't keep those reasons present and clear, if you don't develop clarity in regard to them, inevitably, you're gonna eat out of greed and out of sensuality, out of enjoyment for taste, not need for the nutriments.

486
00:40:53,176 --> 00:40:56,959
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's something you have to responsibly maintain each time you eat the food.

487
00:40:57,680 --> 00:40:59,341
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the whole point of that contemplation.

488
00:40:59,421 --> 00:41:05,506
[SPEAKER_01]: I will eat for the sake of health and sustenance of the body, strength, so that I can continue practicing.

489
00:41:05,546 --> 00:41:07,008
[SPEAKER_01]: I will not eat for beautification.

490
00:41:07,028 --> 00:41:08,469
[SPEAKER_01]: I will not eat for sensual enjoyment.

491
00:41:08,489 --> 00:41:09,430
[SPEAKER_01]: I will not eat for taste.

492
00:41:10,550 --> 00:41:13,933
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's not something that's always written, like decided for you.

493
00:41:13,953 --> 00:41:16,816
[SPEAKER_01]: That's something that you choose to decide every time you go to eat food.

494
00:41:17,416 --> 00:41:21,379
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in this particular instance, all you need to do is be careless of you eating the food,

495
00:41:22,272 --> 00:41:23,673
[SPEAKER_01]: And immediately become central.

496
00:41:24,773 --> 00:41:26,874
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're not careless, it doesn't go into centrality.

497
00:41:27,435 --> 00:41:29,556
[SPEAKER_01]: But people, that's that harder to maintain.

498
00:41:30,556 --> 00:41:34,979
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's much easier to then just say, I'll not eat food, or I'll only eat food that I hate, and so on.

499
00:41:35,259 --> 00:41:35,959
[SPEAKER_01]: That doesn't matter.

500
00:41:35,979 --> 00:41:37,880
[SPEAKER_01]: You're still gonna eat for unwholesome reasons then.

501
00:41:38,641 --> 00:41:42,823
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the unwholesomeness of eating food for unwholesome reasons, it's not in the food.

502
00:41:44,013 --> 00:41:44,994
[SPEAKER_01]: It's in your carelessness.

503
00:41:45,434 --> 00:41:47,315
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in the example of going to a library, no.

504
00:41:47,835 --> 00:41:53,278
[SPEAKER_01]: You can still go to a library if you really need to get the suttas or other books that might help you contemplate further.

505
00:41:53,958 --> 00:41:56,039
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, you do it very, very carefully.

506
00:41:56,920 --> 00:41:59,301
[SPEAKER_01]: Go to the hours when you know there won't be people.

507
00:41:59,341 --> 00:42:01,062
[SPEAKER_01]: If there are people, avoid looking at them.

508
00:42:02,663 --> 00:42:06,005
[SPEAKER_01]: If you have to look at them, avoid talking to them, even at the sake of being rude.

509
00:42:07,466 --> 00:42:10,107
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you know you will not give rise to the unwholesome.

510
00:42:11,537 --> 00:42:20,399
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you just say, I'll never step outside of my house, I'm gonna be locked in this cell for three months, I will not talk to anybody ever again, you're just obscuring the problem.

511
00:42:20,779 --> 00:42:23,339
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not gonna see where the line of your responsibility is.

512
00:42:23,379 --> 00:42:23,619
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

513
00:42:23,639 --> 00:42:26,480
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're denying the whole thing.

514
00:42:26,500 --> 00:42:34,122
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha didn't make actions an offense to engage in a practice of silence, not talking to anybody, ever again.

515
00:42:34,142 --> 00:42:38,523
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they were like those ascetics, the Brahmins, they would just refuse to speak for the rest of their life.

516
00:42:38,543 --> 00:42:40,443
[SPEAKER_01]: They said, well, it's misguided.

517
00:42:46,705 --> 00:42:49,266
[SPEAKER_01]: But most people would just jump on the first excuse.

518
00:42:50,107 --> 00:42:51,267
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, why do I need to go to library?

519
00:42:51,527 --> 00:42:53,368
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, because to get the sutras and that's a good thing.

520
00:42:53,408 --> 00:42:53,968
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, fine.

521
00:42:54,789 --> 00:42:57,290
[SPEAKER_01]: While the real reason remains underneath, right there.

522
00:42:57,390 --> 00:43:04,894
[SPEAKER_01]: But they refuse to look at it, because then it will fill you with guilt, anxiety, sense of responsibility, which is already a burden.

523
00:43:06,894 --> 00:43:07,895
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's where the practice is.

524
00:43:08,675 --> 00:43:09,716
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm doing that burden.

525
00:43:10,376 --> 00:43:14,178
[SPEAKER_01]: But in order to do the burden of responsibility, first you need to accept it, not deny it.

